## 1. Project Architecture
To satisfy the **Component-Based Structure** requirement, you should organize your files like this:

<!-- *   `App.js`: The "Source of Truth" where your state lives.
*   `MovieList.js`: A container that maps through the data.
*   `MovieItem.js`: The visual representation of a single movie.
*   `MovieForm.js`: A component to handle adding new entries. -->



---

## 2. Core Logic (Data & Rendering)
In your `App.js`, you'll initialize your state with an array of objects. To handle **Interactivity**, you'll need the `useState` hook.

<!-- ```javascript
const [movies, setMovies] = useState([
  { id: 1, title: "Inception", genre: "Sci-Fi" },
  { id: 2, title: "The Godfather", genre: "Crime" }
]);
``` -->

### Dynamic Rendering
Inside `MovieList.js`, use the `.map()` function. **Don't forget the `key` prop**—React needs it to track which items change or get deleted.

<!-- ```javascript
{movies.map(movie => (
  <MovieItem 
    key={movie.id} 
    movie={movie} 
    onDelete={deleteMovie} 
  />
))} -->
```

---

## 3. Implementation Checklist

| Feature | Tip for Implementation |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Adding Items** | Use a form with `onChange` to update local state, then pass the new object to the main `movies` state on submit. |
| **Deleting Items** | Use `.filter()` to create a new array excluding the ID of the item you clicked. |
| **Git Commits** | **Don't do one giant commit.** Commit after: Initial setup, Creating the data array, Adding the Map function, Styling the header, etc. |
| **Deployment** | **Vercel** is the easiest for React. Connect your GitHub repo, and it will deploy automatically every time you push. |

---

## 4. Suggested Workflow to Ace the Rubric

1.  **Initialize:** Run `npx create-react-app my-project` and push to GitHub immediately.
2.  **Statics:** Create your mock data array and get it to display on the screen using `.map()`.
3.  **Components:** Break the `map` logic out into a separate `MovieItem` component. (That’s 2 points!).
4.  **Interactivity:** Add a "Delete" button. This is the easiest way to prove you understand state updates.
5.  **Styling:** Use **Flexbox** or **CSS Grid** to make the list look like a clean gallery rather than a plain vertical list.
6.  **The "Commit" Polish:** Even if you finish quickly, go back and make small tweaks to your CSS or README to ensure you hit that **10-commit minimum**.

---

## Pro-Tip for the Bonus
If you want the bonus points for **API Fetching**, swap your static array for a `useEffect` hook that calls the [TMDB API](https://www.themoviedb.org/documentation/api) or even a simple JSON placeholder. It makes the project look much more professional!

How are you planning to handle the styling—standard CSS files, or are you thinking about trying something like Tailwind?